completions_1:
To find the projection matrix, we need to find the orthogonal projection matrix onto the $y$-axis. This is the matrix that multiplies a vector $(x, y)$ to give its projection onto the $y$-axis, $(0, y)$.

The equation of the $y$-axis is $x = 0$. The equation of a line that is perpendicular to the $y$-axis is $y = mx$, where $m$ is the slope. Since $x = 0$ on the $y$-axis, the perpendicular line goes through the origin, so $m = \frac{\text{rise}}{\text{run}} = \frac{0}{1} = 0$.

Let's denote the projection matrix as $P$. We require $P(x, y) = (0, y)$, so a possible solution is
\begin{align*}
P \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix} &= \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ y \end{bmatrix}
\end{align*}

Since this satisfies the requirement, we have discovered the projection matrix onto the $y$-axis. We summarize our result with units:
\boxed{ \text{The projection matrix onto the } y \text{-axis is } \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix} }

completions_5:
To find the matrix that projects onto the y-axis, we first find the standard basis vectors for our space, which are the unit vectors $\vect{i}$ and $\vect{j}$. Since we want to project onto the $y$-axis, the vector we are looking for should be along the $y$-axis. Taking the standard unit vector $\vect{j}$ orthogonal to the $x$-axis and transforming it to a matrix gives the desired result.

Let $\vect{b} = \vect{j} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}.$ In matrix form, this becomes:
\begin{align*}
\boxed{\mathbb{P}} &= \vect{b}\vect{b}^T = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix} \boxed{ \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix} }
\end{align*}

This yields the projection matrix that projects onto the $y$-axis. According to our custom, the output statement must be 1 separated line with the answer enclosed in double brackets:

\boxed{ \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix} }

completions_6:
To solve this problem, we first need to know that the projection onto the $y$-axis is defined as the vector that is parallel to the $y$-axis and through the origin. From the definition of the standard basis vectors ($e_1=(1,0)$ and $e_2=(0,1)$), we know that the vectors parallel to the $y$-axis are multiples of $e_2$, since they only have a non-zero $y$-coordinate. The vector through the origin that is parallel to $e_2$ is just $e_2$ itself.

Thus, the matrix that projects onto the $y$-axis is an matrix with zeros everywhere except for a one in the second position, corresponding to the second basis vector. In matrix form, we can write this as:

\boxed{
    \begin{bmatrix}
        0 & 0 \\
        0 & 1 \\
    \end{bmatrix}
}

This matrix projects any vector onto the $y$-axis.

