The lateral surface area of a cylindrical tube is given by the formula $A = 2\pi rh$. Since the lateral surface area is $48\pi$ square centimeters, we can set up the equation:
\begin{align*}
48\pi &= 2\pi rh \\
48 &= 2rh
\end{align*}

Solving for $r$, we find:
\begin{align*}
r &= \frac{48}{2} \\
r &= 24
\end{align*}

Now that we have the radius, we can find the volume using the formula $V = \pi r^2h$. Plugging in our values:
\begin{align*}
V &= \pi (24)^2 (6) \\
    &= \pi (576) (6) \\
    &= 3456\pi
\end{align*}
\boxed{$\displaystyle 3456\pi \text{ cubic centimeters}$}

