We are looking for the hundreds digit of $(2011^2)^2$.
The hundreds digit depends only on the last two digits of the square of a 3-digit number.
Given $n = 2011$, we can write:
\begin{align*}
 & n^2\\
 &= (1000 + 2011)^2\\
 &= 10^6 + 4044041 + 40242011 + 2 \cdot 1000 \cdot 2011\\
 &= 10^6 + 4044041 + 80484022 + 40222222\\
 &= 141124473 + 10^6\\
\end{align*}

For the last two digits, we can write:
