\begin{align}
r^6 \cos(\theta) + r^3 \cos(\theta) + 1 &= 0 \tag{1} \\
r^6 \sin(\theta) + r^3 \sin(\theta) &= 0 \tag{2}
\end{align}

Equation (2) implies $r^3 \sin(\theta) = 0$. Since $r>0$ (by definition root has no imaginary part), $\sin(\theta)$ must be zero. It gives a trivial solution, $\theta = 0$, which doesn't meet the requirements from the problem statement (i.e., between $90^\circ$ and $180^\circ$). However, we should follow the proof process to the end:

