\text{ (since $r>0$, $\cos(\theta)\in[-1,1]$, $\cos^2(\theta)\leq 1$, $\sin^2(\theta)>0$ for $\theta \in (0^\circ, 180^\circ)$)}
\end{align*}

We can see the discriminant is always greater than zero, so $\phi'(\theta)$ doesn't change its sign as it increases along the interval $(0^\circ,180^\circ)$. So, if the roots argument were $0^\circ$ or $180^\circ$, $\phi(\theta)$ would be either always increasing (and can't be smaller than $90^\circ$) or always decreasing (and can't be greater than $90^\circ)$. Since neither of these is true, the roots argument must be strictly between $0^\circ$ and $180^\circ$.

